{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said to Cain, etc.</b> It is difficult, the verse's saying <b>why are you wroth</b>, etc. — does the Good One become wroth at him because of the extremity of his lowliness in comparison to his younger brother, so that his face fell? And how does He remove his shame by saying הֲלֹא אִם תֵּיטִיב — that is a remedy for the future but not for the past? Further, one must understand the verse's saying <b>at the door sin crouches</b> — it has no [self-evident] meaning, and everything stated in the matter requires explanation." "<b>However</b>, the intent of the word of HaShem at the beginning of His words is to inform him that HaShem took note of the two feelings that had overtaken him, and concerning each one He says לָמָּה, according to all the explanations we gave for his anger and his fallen face. And He gave him the reason and foundation of the matter, to know the knowledge of the Most High and its sod, which is His saying <b>if you do well</b> — meaning: if you are from the aspect of good, all your deeds — the aspect of good rests upon them, and of themselves they are lifted up, for holiness has no need of others. Go and learn (Bava Metzia 85a) from the matter of Rabbi Ḥiyya's chair, which rose on its own upward; and that is His saying <b>lifted up</b> (שְׂאֵת) — meaning, of itself. And שְׂאֵת can be interpreted both of the offering and of Cain himself — that his face should not fall, for he is a comely image, but rather his elevation should be lifted up, which is the aspect of good, which is the aspect of exaltedness and greatness; and these correspond to the two aspects mentioned in the matter ״וַיִּשַׁע ה׳ אֶל הֶבֶל וְאֶל מִנְחָתוֹ״ (Genesis 4:4), which are two things. <b>And if you do not do well</b> — meaning: when you divest your mind of the aspect of good — <b>at the door</b>, meaning: at your beginning, you are accounted evil; behold, the aspect of evil, which is called חַטָּאת, crouches upon you and upon all your deeds, and strips from you the lifting up, and rules over your deeds. And from this, what place is there for your anger, since you yourself are the cause of the matter?" "And the verse's saying <b>and its desire is toward you</b> — here He alluded that the evil inclination has in its nature an aspect of desire to cause a person to sin, just as it is in a person's nature to crave a craving and to lust after gold. And the verse's saying <b>and you shall rule over it</b> — here He informed him that even though it is in this order, it is nevertheless in his hand to rule over it. And from this, why should your face fall, since the matter is in your hand to rule over it and to remove the stumbling block, which is the sin?" "<b>Further</b>, it may intend to say along this line: <b>And if you do not do well, at the door</b> — which is the gate of acceptance of offerings and gifts — there crouches the sin, so that there be no place for the gift to ascend, and needless to say it has no power to be lifted up. And if you say: You are the Master, the Ruler — accept the offering, and the sin will not prevent it — therefore He said: It is true that if you do repentance there is place for this claim; but since you are not thinking of repentance, as alluded to in the word ״לֹא תֵיטִיב״ — that you still crave and desire the aspect of evil — and that is His saying <b>and its desire is toward you</b>, and you wish to rule over it and banish it so that it not crouch at your door: even though HaShem wishes to separate it from you, still you must distance it from your desire, for as long as a person desires the aspect of evil, he empowers it over himself, as it says (Psalms 81:10): ״לֹא יִהְיֶה בְךָ אֵל זָר״. And when a person spurns its things and its counsel, that is its debasement, as it says (Proverbs 16:32): ״וּמוֹשֵׁל בְּרוּחוֹ מִלֹּכֵד עִיר״. Accordingly, HaShem speaks to him in a tone of wonder, as He began to speak to him ״לָמָּה״ etc. and ״לָמָּה״ etc., for his complaint is against himself and not against his King — for how can he wish to rule over it when he himself empowers it over himself? Understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:39:30.237897980Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}